choose your vehicle  trying to buy an ev in the us right now  is like trying to choose video game  character see they all have different  traits and qualities but some of them  have got this additional federal  discount option unlocked while others  haven't it's because there's legislation  that decides which cars are eligible for  a federal discount and which ones aren't  but here's the big thing that  legislation could be about to change in  a big way it's called the inflation  reduction act of 2022. it's a big deal  see the senate just passed a climate  bill that is set to completely redefine  which cars qualify for a federal  discount it's like a cheat code that's  rewriting the rules of the game  unlocking a whole new level of  previously excluded evs but at the same  time it could shut some automakers out  of the game entirely so who are the  winners and losers of this eevee  discount let's go  okay first we need to establish why some  cars qualify for the existing federal  discount and others don't it all starts  with the energy improvement and  extension act of 2008 which attempted to  encourage adoption of plug-in vehicles  by offering federal tax credits up to  7500  on qualified plug-in electric drive  motor vehicles the credit applied to any  new plug-in vehicle but not second-hand  ones and it wasn't applied when you  bought the car but instead came off your  tax bill there was also a cap that meant  the amount of credit was phased out  after an automaker sold 200 000  qualifying vehicles so as soon as tesla  sold its 200 000 car which happened  years ago uh that started to phase out  and now someone who buys a tesla doesn't  get any credit at all and that's also  happened in general motors and just  recently toyota it's that cap that's  been the source of frustration for some  automakers their argument has been we  were early on in eb sales we helped sort  of create this market why are we getting  penalized now but all of that is set to  change under this new legislation  firstly the 200 000 car cap is gone so  tesla gm and toyota would be back in the  game and the legislation now includes a  wider range of vehicles listen closely  it also gives consumers a tax credit to  buy  any electric vehicle or fuel cell  vehicle new or used and a tax credit for  up to seven thousand five hundred  dollars if those vehicles were made in  america let me break that down firstly  the new legislation extends the tax  credits to used vehicles too a whole  extra level of cars that were previously  excluded however you won't get the full  7 500 off a used car instead it's maxed  out at four thousand dollars the used  car market is a lot bigger than the new  car market it's like 70 percent of car  sales actually are exchanged on the used  market the new credit also includes  hydrogen fuel cell cars too and not just  battery evs and instead of waiting for  the discount to appear on your taxes the  credit is applied when you buy the car  but with this new approach comes new  eligibility criteria and it's this new  criteria that's really shaking things up  firstly to qualify the car would have to  be assembled in north america and then  there's restrictions over how much money  you make and how much the car costs on a  car i think it's 55 000  uh you know anything beyond that from a  sticker price standpoint  wouldn't qualify for a truck it's 80 000  and then there's income limits too  anyone with the income over 150 000 300  000 for uh married couples don't qualify  either that has been a bit of a sticking  point for some of the luxury players and  um startups like rivian rivian came out  and said almost none of our cars will  qualify now because their trucks start  around that 80 000 price tag but there's  one part of this legislation that could  render many of these vehicles ineligible  for the discount and that's new rules  that state that at least half of your  battery must be made or processed in the  united states or by one of its free  trade partners they want the industry  off of the reliance on china which is a  huge step to take because any battery  that comes in an eb is going to have  stuff that was either extracted or  processed or in some way touched by  china and this bill says if that's the  case then you don't qualify now those  battery requirements are set to come in  at the start of 2024.  that gives automakers just under a year  and a half to bolster their us battery  production or attempt to get that figure  changed so what does this mean if you're  trying to choose an ev right now well if  you're after a tesla or one of the other  brands that have been capped or you're  in the market for a secondhand dv it  might be worth waiting to see if the  house passes this bill as it could save  you money but if you're fortunate enough  to live in one of those wealthy  households or you're looking to buy a  high ndv  you might want to get in there before  the rules of the game get completely  rewritten  [Music]  you

